<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vincent Ogé</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1757–<a href="/years/1791">1791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vincent Ogé was a merchant, military officer and revolutionary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> who led a failed uprising against French colonial rule in 1790, shortly before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>. Born at Dondon to a white French father and a free woman of colour, he became the richest merchant of African descent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Français</a> and part-owner of his family's slave plantation. In revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> he campaigned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julien-raimond">Julien Raimond</a> for the rights of free people of colour, while supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. When the colony refused to apply the law of March 1790, he and Jean-Baptiste Chavannes raised about 300 free men of colour; captured, he was broken on the wheel on 6 February 1791.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vincent-oge.jpg" alt="Vincent Ogé" width="330" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fouquet - The Louvre project · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_drawing_of_Vincent_Ogé_(1790).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wealthy Creole planters saw a chance of independence; <b>Vincent Ogé</b>, Jean-Baptiste Chavannes and others incited revolts against the royal government, among them a slave revolt that laid waste the northern plain, and the Creoles soon lost control of it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1790 <b>Vincent Ogé</b>, a wealthy merchant of colour, rose to claim the rights he believed the National Assembly had granted; he was broken on the wheel in 1791.</blockquote><a href="/societies/saint-domingue">Social Hierarchy of Saint-Domingue</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Haïtien</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.1,-72.33&amp;year=1779" aria-label="Saint-Domingue on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2594 1456 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vincent Ogé"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3044 1726h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3044 1726h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.1,-72.33&amp;year=1779">What was here in 1779? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
